# OOP in Java - Lecture 5: Polymorphism

These notes summarize the key ideas of polymorphism in Java. They are designed for classroom study and repository use, not a verbatim transcript of the lecture video.

## 1) What is polymorphism?

The word polymorphism means "many forms".

In Java, polymorphism allows one method or object to behave differently depending on the context.

The two main types are:

- compile-time polymorphism
- runtime polymorphism

## 2) Compile-time polymorphism: method overloading

Method overloading happens when multiple methods in the same class have the same name but different parameters.

```java
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}
```

This is resolved at compile time by the Java compiler based on the number and types of arguments.

### Benefits

- same method name, different behavior
- cleaner API design
- easier readability

## 3) Runtime polymorphism: method overriding

Runtime polymorphism occurs when a child class provides a specific implementation of a method already defined in a parent class.

```java
class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}
```

When an `Animal` reference points to a `Dog` object, Java calls the `Dog` version of `sound()` at runtime.

This is called dynamic method dispatch.

## 4) Upcasting

A subclass object can be assigned to a parent type reference.

```java
Animal a = new Dog();
```

This is allowed because a `Dog` is an `Animal`.

The important part is that the actual object type is still `Dog`, so Java decides which overridden method to call dynamically.

## 5) `@Override` annotation

The `@Override` annotation tells the compiler:

- this method is intended to override a parent method
- if the signature does not match, Java gives an error

It is a good Java practice to use it.

## 6) Why polymorphism is useful

Polymorphism helps us:

- write flexible code
- reduce repetitive conditions
- handle different objects through a common parent type
- create cleaner class hierarchies

Example:

```java
Animal[] animals = {new Dog(), new Cat(), new Cow()};

for (Animal animal : animals) {
    animal.sound();
}
```

Each object calls its own `sound()` implementation.

## 7) `instanceof` keyword

`instanceof` checks whether an object belongs to a particular class or subclass.

```java
if (animal instanceof Dog) {
    System.out.println("This is a dog");
}
```

This is useful when you need to handle specific subclass behavior.

## 8) Abstract classes and polymorphism

An abstract class can define common behavior while leaving some behavior to subclasses.

```java
abstract class Shape {
    abstract void draw();
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing a circle");
    }
}
```

This promotes a common contract for all shapes while allowing each shape to implement its own details.

## 9) Real-world example

A payment system can use polymorphism:

- `Payment` is the parent class
- `UPI`, `Card`, and `Cash` are child classes
- each one implements `pay()` differently

This makes code easier to expand later.

## 10) Key takeaways

- Polymorphism means "many forms".
- Overloading gives compile-time polymorphism.
- Overriding gives runtime polymorphism.
- Parent references can point to child objects.
- Java decides the method version at runtime.
- `@Override` helps prevent mistakes.
- Polymorphism makes code reusable and extensible.

## 11) Interview-style summary

Be ready to explain:

- What is polymorphism?
- Difference between method overloading and overriding
- What is dynamic method dispatch?
- Why is upcasting useful?
- How does Java decide which method to call?
- When would you use `instanceof`?

